Use `std::stod` inside a `try`/`catch` block. It throws:

- `std::invalid_argument` if no conversion can be performed.
- `std::out_of_range` if the value is outside the range accepted for `double` (including overflow, and often underflow/very tiny values).

Example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>

int main()
{
    std::string s = "1e500";

    try {
        std::size_t pos = 0;
        double value = std::stod(s, &pos);

        if (pos != s.size()) {
            std::cout << "Extra characters after the number.\n";
        }

        if (!std::isfinite(value)) {
            std::cout << "Value is inf or nan.\n";
        }

        std::cout << "Converted: " << value << '\n';
    }
    catch (const std::out_of_range&) {
        std::cout << "Number is out of range for double.\n";
    }
    catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {
        std::cout << "String does not contain a valid number.\n";
    }
}
```

If you do not want exceptions, you can use `std::strtod` and check `errno`, or use `std::from_chars` where supported.

