My answer to the user prompt:

The immediate `printWindow.close()` is closing the popup before the browser has finished opening the print preview/print dialog. The safer approach is to close the window only after the print flow has completed, preferably using the `afterprint` event.

Also, avoid `document.open('text/plain')` if you are writing HTML. If you only want to print plain text, wrap it in `<pre>` inside a normal HTML document.

Example:

```javascript
const printWindow = window.open('', '_blank');

if (!printWindow) {
  alert('Please allow popups to print.');
  return;
}

printWindow.document.open();
printWindow.document.write(`
  <!doctype html>
  <html>
    <head>
      <title>Print</title>
    </head>
    <body>
      <pre>toto</pre>
    </body>
  </html>
`);
printWindow.document.close();

const closePrintWindow = function () {
  if (printWindow && !printWindow.closed) {
    printWindow.close();
  }
};

// Important: register this before calling print()
printWindow.onafterprint = closePrintWindow;

printWindow.focus();
printWindow.print();
```

If your printed content contains images, external CSS, fonts, or dynamically loaded content, wait until that content has loaded before calling `print()`:

```javascript
printWindow.onload = function () {
  printWindow.focus();
  printWindow.print();
};
```

For very old browsers that do not reliably support `afterprint`, a fallback timeout can be used, but it is less reliable because you do not really know when the user has finished with the print dialog:

```javascript
setTimeout(closePrintWindow, 1000);
```

So the main fix is: do not call `printWindow.close()` synchronously right after `printWindow.print()`; close it in `onafterprint`, and attach that handler before calling `print()`.

Evaluation of Assistant A and Assistant B:

Assistant A correctly identifies the root cause: the window is being closed too early. The suggested timeout approach can work in some cases, but it is not ideal because it is arbitrary and may close the window too early or too late depending on the browser and user behavior.

Assistant A’s `onafterprint` example has an important issue: it assigns `printWindow.onafterprint` after calling `printWindow.print()`. In some browsers, `print()` may block or the event may be dispatched before the later assignment runs, so the handler may never be used. The handler should be registered before calling `print()`.

Assistant A’s note about removing `'text/plain'` is useful, but the answer would be stronger with a complete HTML example and a note about waiting for external resources if needed.

Assistant B gives the better core solution: register `onafterprint` before calling `print()`, then do not call `printWindow.close()` synchronously. The example writes a complete HTML document, which is more reliable than using `document.open('text/plain')`. Assistant B also correctly notes that `afterprint` does not mean the physical printer has finished printing, and that images or stylesheets should be loaded before printing.

Assistant B’s answer could be improved by mentioning a fallback for browsers where `afterprint` is not reliable, and by mentioning popup blocking, but it is still more accurate and safer than Assistant A’s answer.

My final verdict is Assistant B is slightly better: [[B>A]]

