My answer to your prompt:

`endPos` is a `Vector3`, which is a value type. Once you assign it, it should keep that value unless you assign it again. The real issue is that your “start” value is not fixed.

This line is the main problem:

```csharp
startMarker = this.transform;
```

`startMarker` is not storing the starting position. It is storing a reference to the same `Transform` you are moving. So when the object moves, `startMarker.position` also changes. That makes this line behave incorrectly:

```csharp
transform.position = Vector3.Lerp(startMarker.position, endPos, journeyAmount);
```

Instead of lerping from the original start position to `endPos`, you are lerping from the current moving position to `endPos`.

You also have a few other issues:

1. `journeyAmount` is calculated before `distance` is updated.
2. `distance` is never reset when starting a new movement.
3. `StopCoroutine(move())` does not stop the already running coroutine because `move()` creates a new enumerator.
4. If `moveToCenter()` is called multiple times, multiple coroutines can run at once.
5. If the start and end positions are the same, `journeyLength` can be zero, causing division by zero.
6. You are also using `startMarker.rotation`, which changes while the object rotates. You should store the starting rotation too.

A corrected version would look like this:

```csharp
using System.Collections;
using UnityEngine;

public class moveCard : MonoBehaviour
{
    public storage Storage;
    public float speed = 20f;
    public int dir = 1;

    private Coroutine moveRoutine;

    void Awake()
    {
        if (gameObject.name == "reference")
        {
            Storage.movePos = transform.position;
        }
    }

    public void moveToCenter()
    {
        // Stop any existing movement coroutine before starting a new one.
        if (moveRoutine != null)
        {
            StopCoroutine(moveRoutine);
            moveRoutine = null;
        }

        if (speed <= 0f)
        {
            Debug.LogWarning("speed must be greater than zero.");
            return;
        }

        Vector3 targetPos = new Vector3(
            Storage.movePos.x * dir,
            Storage.movePos.y,
            Storage.movePos.z
        );

        moveRoutine = StartCoroutine(MoveToPosition(targetPos));
    }

    private IEnumerator MoveToPosition(Vector3 targetPos)
    {
        // Store the starting values as copies, not as moving references.
        Vector3 startPos = transform.position;
        Quaternion startRot = transform.rotation;
        Quaternion targetRot = Quaternion.Euler(Storage.rot);

        float journeyLength = Vector3.Distance(startPos, targetPos);

        // If we are already at the target, just snap and stop.
        if (journeyLength <= 0f)
        {
            transform.position = targetPos;
            transform.rotation = targetRot;
            moveRoutine = null;
            yield break;
        }

        float startTime = Time.time;

        while (true)
        {
            float distanceCovered = (Time.time - startTime) * speed;
            float journeyAmount = Mathf.Clamp01(distanceCovered / journeyLength);

            transform.position = Vector3.Lerp(startPos, targetPos, journeyAmount);
            transform.rotation = Quaternion.Lerp(startRot, targetRot, journeyAmount);

            if (journeyAmount >= 1f)
            {
                // Make sure we end exactly at the target.
                transform.position = targetPos;
                transform.rotation = targetRot;
                break;
            }

            yield return null;
        }

        moveRoutine = null;
    }
}
```

The important fix is this part:

```csharp
Vector3 startPos = transform.position;
Quaternion startRot = transform.rotation;
```

Now the coroutine interpolates from fixed starting values:

```csharp
transform.position = Vector3.Lerp(startPos, targetPos, journeyAmount);
transform.rotation = Quaternion.Lerp(startRot, targetRot, journeyAmount);
```

Instead of from values that change while the object moves.

Also, you probably do not need this:

```csharp
Invoke("stopAnimation", 2f);
```

The coroutine can simply stop itself when the movement is complete.

