My answer to the user prompt:

The likely issue is not that `endPos` is failing to store its value. `endPos` is a `Vector3`, and once assigned it will keep that value until you assign it again.

The main problem is here:

```csharp
startMarker = this.transform;
```

This does not store the starting position. It stores a reference to the same `Transform` component that you are moving. So when this line runs:

```csharp
transform.position = Vector3.Lerp(startMarker.position, endPos, journeyAmount);
```

`startMarker.position` is also changing every frame because `startMarker` is the same object as `transform`.

That makes the interpolation behave incorrectly and can make it look like `endPos` is not being kept, even though `endPos` is fine.

You should store the starting position and starting rotation as values:

```csharp
Vector3 startPos = transform.position;
Quaternion startRot = transform.rotation;
```

There are also several other issues in the original code:

1. `distance` is not reset before a new movement.
2. `journeyAmount` is calculated before `distance` is updated.
3. `StopCoroutine(move())` does not stop the already-running coroutine because it creates a new enumerator.
4. Calling `moveToCenter()` multiple times can start multiple coroutines.
5. If the start and end positions are the same, `journeyLength` can be zero, causing a division-by-zero problem.

A corrected version:

```csharp
using System.Collections;
using UnityEngine;

public class moveCard : MonoBehaviour
{
    public storage Storage;
    public float speed = 20f;
    public int dir = 1;

    private Coroutine moveRoutine;

    void Awake()
    {
        if (gameObject.name == "reference")
        {
            Storage.movePos = transform.position;
        }
    }

    public void moveToCenter()
    {
        if (moveRoutine != null)
        {
            StopCoroutine(moveRoutine);
            moveRoutine = null;
        }

        Vector3 targetPos = new Vector3(
            Storage.movePos.x * dir,
            Storage.movePos.y,
            Storage.movePos.z
        );

        Quaternion targetRot = Quaternion.Euler(Storage.rot);

        moveRoutine = StartCoroutine(Move(targetPos, targetRot));
    }

    private IEnumerator Move(Vector3 targetPos, Quaternion targetRot)
    {
        Vector3 startPos = transform.position;
        Quaternion startRot = transform.rotation;

        float journeyLength = Vector3.Distance(startPos, targetPos);

        if (journeyLength <= 0.0001f)
        {
            transform.position = targetPos;
            transform.rotation = targetRot;
            moveRoutine = null;
            yield break;
        }

        float startTime = Time.time;

        while (true)
        {
            float distanceTravelled = (Time.time - startTime) * speed;
            float journeyAmount = Mathf.Clamp01(distanceTravelled / journeyLength);

            transform.position = Vector3.Lerp(startPos, targetPos, journeyAmount);
            transform.rotation = Quaternion.Lerp(startRot, targetRot, journeyAmount);

            if (journeyAmount >= 1f)
            {
                transform.position = targetPos;
                transform.rotation = targetRot;
                break;
            }

            yield return null;
        }

        moveRoutine = null;
    }
}
```

The key fix is replacing this:

```csharp
startMarker = this.transform;
```

with stored values:

```csharp
Vector3 startPos = transform.position;
Quaternion startRot = transform.rotation;
```

Then use those stored values inside the coroutine:

```csharp
transform.position = Vector3.Lerp(startPos, targetPos, journeyAmount);
transform.rotation = Quaternion.Lerp(startRot, targetRot, journeyAmount);
```

