C++ normally does not use a tracing garbage collector for linked lists. Instead, the idiomatic “automatic garbage collection” is RAII with smart pointers. For a singly linked list, each node can own its `next` node using `std::unique_ptr`. When the list is destroyed or a node is removed, the memory is freed automatically.

Below is a robust example using `std::unique_ptr`. It also clears the list iteratively in the destructor to avoid deep recursive destruction for very long lists.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <utility>

template <typename T>
class LinkedList {
private:
    struct Node {
        T value;
        std::unique_ptr<Node> next;

        explicit Node(T v) : value(std::move(v)) {}
    };

    std::unique_ptr<Node> head;

public:
    LinkedList() = default;

    // The list owns its nodes uniquely, so disable copying.
    LinkedList(const LinkedList&) = delete;
    LinkedList& operator=(const LinkedList&) = delete;

    // Clean up iteratively to avoid recursive destruction of a long list.
    ~LinkedList() {
        clear();
    }

    void push_front(T value) {
        auto new_node = std::make_unique<Node>(std::move(value));
        new_node->next = std::move(head);
        head = std::move(new_node);
    }

    void pop_front() {
        if (!head) {
            return;
        }

        auto old_head = std::move(head);
        head = std::move(old_head->next);

        // old_head is destroyed here, freeing only the old head node.
    }

    void clear() {
        while (head) {
            pop_front();
        }
    }

    bool empty() const {
        return head == nullptr;
    }

    void print() const {
        for (const Node* current = head.get();
             current != nullptr;
             current = current->next.get()) {
            std::cout << current->value << " -> ";
        }
        std::cout << "nullptr\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    LinkedList<int> list;

    list.push_front(30);
    list.push_front(20);
    list.push_front(10);

    list.print(); // 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> nullptr

    list.pop_front();
    list.print(); // 20 -> 30 -> nullptr

    list.clear();
    list.print(); // nullptr

    return 0;
} // Any remaining nodes would be freed automatically here.
```

Compile with C++14 or later because `std::make_unique` was introduced in C++14:

```bash
g++ -std=c++14 main.cpp -o main
```

For a doubly linked list, avoid using owning pointers in both directions. Usually `next` should be the owning pointer, while `prev` should be a non-owning raw pointer or `std::weak_ptr` if shared ownership is involved. Otherwise, ownership cycles can prevent automatic cleanup.

