My answer to the user’s prompt:

There is no single standard C++ atomic operation that means “if `i < x`, increment `i`” when `i` and `x` are separate variables. You normally implement it with a compare-and-swap loop.

If `x` is constant, or if using a snapshot of `x` is acceptable:

```cpp
#include <atomic>

bool increment_if_below(std::atomic<int>& i, int x)
{
    int current = i.load();

    while (current < x) {
        if (i.compare_exchange_weak(current, current + 1)) {
            return true;
        }
        // If CAS fails, current is automatically updated to the current value of i.
    }

    return false;
}
```

If `x` is also atomic, but a snapshot of `x` is still acceptable:

```cpp
#include <atomic>

bool increment_if_below(std::atomic<int>& i, const std::atomic<int>& x)
{
    int limit = x.load();
    int current = i.load();

    while (current < limit) {
        if (i.compare_exchange_weak(current, current + 1)) {
            return true;
        }
    }

    return false;
}
```

However, if `x` can change concurrently and the check `i < x` must be atomic with respect to changes to both `i` and `x`, then two separate `std::atomic<int>` objects are not enough. You need either a mutex protecting both variables, or one atomic object containing both values:

```cpp
#include <atomic>

struct State {
    int i;
    int x;
};

std::atomic<State> state{State{0, 10}};

bool increment_if_below()
{
    State current = state.load();

    while (current.i < current.x) {
        State next = current;
        ++next.i;

        if (state.compare_exchange_weak(current, next)) {
            return true;
        }
    }

    return false;
}
```

The combined-state approach makes the condition and increment one atomic compare-exchange operation on the whole state. The downside is that `std::atomic<State>` is not guaranteed to be lock-free on every platform. If it is not lock-free, or if the state becomes larger or more complicated, a mutex is often the simpler and more portable solution.

