You can do this in two main ways.

## 1. Use multiple `SecurityFilterChain` beans selected by `Referer`

This is the cleanest approach when different referers should get different security configurations or different sets of filters.

Spring Security chooses the **first matching** `SecurityFilterChain`, so you should use `@Order` and provide a fallback chain.

Example for Spring Boot 3 / Spring Security 6:

```java
import java.net.URI;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.core.annotation.Order;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.configuration.EnableWebSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;
import org.springframework.security.web.authentication.UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter;
import org.springframework.security.web.util.matcher.RequestMatcher;

import static org.springframework.security.config.Customizer.withDefaults;

@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    @Order(1)
    public SecurityFilterChain appAChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .securityMatcher(refererHost("app-a.example.com"))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .addFilterBefore(new AppAFilter(), UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)
            .formLogin(withDefaults());

        return http.build();
    }

    @Bean
    @Order(2)
    public SecurityFilterChain appBChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .securityMatcher(refererHost("app-b.example.com"))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .addFilterBefore(new AppBFilter(), UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)
            .formLogin(withDefaults());

        return http.build();
    }

    @Bean
    @Order(3)
    public SecurityFilterChain defaultChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .formLogin(withDefaults());

        return http.build();
    }

    private static RequestMatcher refererHost(String expectedHost) {
        return request -> {
            String referer = request.getHeader("Referer");

            if (referer == null) {
                return false;
            }

            try {
                URI uri = URI.create(referer);
                return expectedHost.equalsIgnoreCase(uri.getHost());
            } catch (IllegalArgumentException ex) {
                return false;
            }
        };
    }
}
```

If you need an exact referer match instead of host matching, you can do:

```java
private static RequestMatcher refererEquals(String expectedReferer) {
    return request -> expectedReferer.equals(request.getHeader("Referer"));
}
```

Important points:

- The first matching chain wins.
- Chains do not combine. If `appAChain` matches, Spring Security does not also apply `defaultChain`.
- Always include a fallback chain unless you intentionally want unmatched requests to bypass Spring Security.
- Do not use naive `contains()` or `startsWith()` matching on `Referer`; it can be spoofed or accidentally match malicious values.
- The `Referer` header is client-controlled and may be absent due to browser referrer policies. Do not use it as a trusted authentication or authorization signal.

## 2. Use one chain and make filters conditionally skip themselves

If only the filter behavior differs, but the rest of the security configuration is the same, it may be simpler to use one `SecurityFilterChain` and let each filter decide whether it should run.

Example:

```java
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import org.springframework.web.filter.OncePerRequestFilter;

public class AppAFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    @Override
    protected boolean shouldNotFilter(HttpServletRequest request) {
        String referer = request.getHeader("Referer");
        return referer == null || !referer.startsWith("https://app-a.example.com");
    }

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(
            HttpServletRequest request,
            jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse response,
            jakarta.servlet.FilterChain filterChain
    ) throws Exception {
        // App A filter logic
        filterChain.doFilter(request, response);
    }
}
```

Then register both filters in the same security chain.

This approach avoids multiple `SecurityFilterChain` beans if the only difference is which custom filter runs.

## Additional warning about Spring bean filters

If your custom filter is annotated with `@Component` or exposed as a Spring bean, Spring Boot may register it as a normal servlet filter in addition to adding it to the Spring Security chain. That can cause the filter to run twice.

Either instantiate the filter directly in the security configuration, or disable servlet registration:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.web.servlet.FilterRegistrationBean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;

@Bean
public FilterRegistrationBean<AppAFilter> disableAppAFilterRegistration(AppAFilter filter) {
    FilterRegistrationBean<AppAFilter> registration = new FilterRegistrationBean<>(filter);
    registration.setEnabled(false);
    return registration;
}
```

