    <article>
      <p>
        The origin of the Romanians is a problem that behaves like a badly folded map: every time one corner is smoothed, another bulges. Romanian is unmistakably a Romance language, descended from the Latin of the Roman world rather than from a later imitation. Yet the territory of modern Romania was not uniformly included in Roman Dacia, and Roman rule north of the Danube lasted only from Trajan’s conquest in the early second century to Aurelian’s withdrawal in the late third. That short interval had to produce, if the simplest story is true, a durable Latin-speaking population capable of surviving centuries of migration, state formation, and linguistic change. If it did not, then the ancestors of Romanian speakers may have formed south of the Danube, in the Romanized Balkans, and moved north later. The debate is therefore not about whether Latin mattered, but about where, when, and how a Latin-speaking world became Romanian.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Daco-Roman continuity argument holds that Romanized inhabitants remained north of the Danube after the imperial administration left. Its supporters point to the obvious difficulty of evacuating an entire provincial society, especially when armies and officials are easier to move than farmers, herders, and townspeople. Archaeology adds a partial case: some settlements, roads, coins, and objects suggest that forms of provincial life did not simply vanish when the province ceased to exist. The survival of certain place-names and the later presence of Latin-speaking groups in the region are also enlisted as clues. In its strongest form, this theory imagines a long, quiet persistence of Romance speech in villages, valleys, and pastoral networks that left few documents. In its weaker and more plausible form, it merely argues that some Romanized communities survived in parts of present-day Romania and contributed to the eventual Romanian-speaking world. The difficulty is that silence in the sources is not evidence of absence, but it is also not a census.
      </p>

      <p>
        The main alternative is usually called the immigrationist hypothesis. It proposes that the Romanian language and people were formed primarily south of the Danube, among Latin-speaking populations of the Balkans, and that their ancestors moved northward into present-day Romania during the early Middle Ages. This view has respectable ingredients: Latin was not confined to Dacia, the Danube was crossed constantly, and Romanian has affinities with Aromanian, Megleno-Romanian, and Istro-Romanian. Some linguistic evidence, including shared Balkan features and contacts with Albanian, also encourages a wider southeastern European setting. The theory became politically sharp in the nineteenth century, especially in the work of Robert Roesler, who argued that Romanians arrived late in Transylvania. That version was useful to critics of Romanian national claims, because a late arrival could weaken arguments about prior settlement and political rights. Still, not every migrationist argument is a political weapon, and the basic fact of movement across the Danube is historically unremarkable. The problem is turning plausible mobility into a single, total replacement story.
      </p>

      <p>
        A less dramatic solution is that both sides are partly right. Some Latin-speaking communities may have remained north of the Danube, while others continued to live south of it, with movement, intermarriage, and pastoral exchange connecting the two zones. This admigration model does not require a deserted Dacia or a sudden medieval transfer of an entire people. It fits a region in which the Danube served less as a wall than as a highway with tolls, raids, ferries, and opportunists. The evidence, however, is stubbornly indirect. A coin can travel farther than a language, a grave cannot always declare its owner’s speech, and a chronicler’s label may reveal more about the writer than the person described. The first secure written references to Vlachs in the region become clearer only in the high Middle Ages, long after the crucial centuries of formation. So the honest answer is not a neat certificate of origin but a probability built from fragments.
      </p>

      <p>
        The argument has never been innocent of politics because it touches Transylvania, a region contested by national memories. Romanian intellectuals in the Habsburg lands, especially the Transylvanian School, stressed Roman descent to claim dignity, antiquity, and rights for a population excluded from privileged political status. For them, continuity was not merely archaeology; it was a legal and moral argument dressed in classical costume. Hungarian, Austrian, and German writers, meanwhile, sometimes favored migration theories because they could portray Romanians as latecomers with weaker historical title to the land. In the twentieth century, Romanian state narratives often made continuity a foundation myth, while communist nationalism under Nicolae Ceaușescu inflated Dacian and Roman antiquity into a spectacle of primordial greatness. Neighboring nationalisms had their own uses for the same past, treating medieval chronicles and place-names as exhibits in a courtroom. This does not mean every scholar was a propagandist, but it does mean that every shard of pottery and every etymology was liable to be drafted into service. The past, in this region, has often been asked to carry more luggage than it can comfortably bear.
      </p>

      <p>
        The most defensible account is neither a pure continuity myth nor a total immigration story. Romanian emerged from the Latin-speaking world of southeastern Europe, shaped by contact with Slavic speakers and by centuries of social change. Some Romanized population probably remained north of the Danube, but no one can yet trace an uninterrupted, document-by-document line from Roman Dacia to modern Romania. Likewise, southern Balkan influence and movement across the river are real, but they do not prove that present-day Romania was empty until medieval migrants arrived. The question should also be separated from modern citizenship: ancient residence does not confer rights today, and late arrival does not cancel belonging. Present-day Romania was made by Romanians, Hungarians, Germans, Roma, Jews, Slavs, Turks, and others, not by a single ancestral tribe. The origin debate is valuable when it sharpens evidence, and misleading when it becomes a nationality test. The Danube, like history, is better treated as a moving waterway than as a border drawn in ink.
      </p>
