## 3. Best long-term design: unify the account identity

If `DoctorAccount` and `UserAccount` are both fundamentally accounts with common fields like:

- `id`
- `phone`
- `passwordHash`
- `fullName`

then the best long-term design is usually not to add more and more separate account tables.

A better model is:

```text
account
-------
id
phone
password_hash
full_name
account_type

doctor_profile
--------------
account_id
specialty_id

user_profile
------------
account_id
...
```

Then you can have one global `Account` entity and one `AccountRepository`.

Example:

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "account")
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.JOINED)
@DiscriminatorColumn(name = "account_type")
public abstract class Account {

    @Id
    private Integer id;

    @Column(unique = true)
    private String phone;

    private String passwordHash;

    private String fullName;

    // getters and setters
}
```

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "doctor_profile")
@DiscriminatorValue("DOCTOR")
public class DoctorAccount extends Account {

    private Integer specialtyId;

    // getters and setters
}
```

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "user_profile")
@DiscriminatorValue("USER")
public class UserAccount extends Account {

    // getters and setters
}
```

Then:

```java
public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, Integer> {
}
```

And the service becomes simple:

```java
@Service
public class AccountService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepository;

    public AccountService(AccountRepository accountRepository) {
        this.accountRepository = accountRepository;
    }

    public Account getById(Integer id) {
        return accountRepository.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new EntityNotFoundException(
                        "Account not found: " + id
                ));
    }
}
```

This gives you:

- globally unique account IDs
- one repository lookup
- easier authentication/login logic
- easier future account types
- cleaner domain model

If you cannot change the schema now, use the lookup chain. If you can change the schema, prefer a common account table or JPA inheritance.

