A common fast implementation aligns the pointer, then scans one `size_t` word at a time and uses a bit trick to detect whether any byte in the word is zero.

Assuming 8-bit bytes, a good implementation is:

```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <limits.h>

#if CHAR_BIT != 8
#error "This version assumes 8-bit bytes."
#endif

size_t fast_strlen(const char *s)
{
    const unsigned char *p = (const unsigned char *)s;
    const unsigned char *start = p;

    /* 0x01010101... */
    const size_t ones = (size_t)-1 / (size_t)0xff;

    /* 0x80808080... */
    const size_t highs = ones << 7;

    /* Handle bytes until we reach a size_t-aligned address. */
    while ((uintptr_t)p % sizeof(size_t) != 0) {
        if (*p == '\0')
            return (size_t)(p - start);
        ++p;
    }

    for (;;) {
        size_t word;

        /*
         * memcpy avoids strict-aliasing problems. Optimizing compilers
         * usually turn this fixed-size memcpy into a single word load.
         */
        memcpy(&word, p, sizeof(word));

        /*
         * Nonzero iff some byte in 'word' is zero.
         *
         * For each byte lane:
         *   - subtracting 1 from 0 causes a borrow and sets the high bit
         *   - ~word keeps the high bit only where the original byte was 0
         */
        if ((word - ones) & ~word & highs) {
            /* Find the first zero byte within the word. */
            for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof(word); ++i) {
                if (p[i] == '\0')
                    return (size_t)(p - start) + i;
            }
        }

        p += sizeof(word);
    }
}
```

The key test is:

```c
(word - ones) & ~word & highs
```

This detects a zero byte inside the whole word without checking each byte individually. Once such a word is found, scanning at most `sizeof(size_t)` bytes finds the exact terminator.

Important caveat: this style of `strlen` may read bytes after the terminating null byte, within the same aligned word. In strictly standard C, that can be undefined behavior if those bytes are outside the string’s allocated object. It is safe only under platform/ABI assumptions where such aligned word reads are valid, or if you know the string has sufficient readable padding. For strictly portable C, use the standard `strlen`.

