## My answer to the prompt

A good refactor should move the multi-call API setup out of the spec file and into dedicated layers:

1. **API types / contracts** – request and response models.
2. **API adapter / gateway** – knows Playwright `APIRequestContext` and endpoints.
3. **Service / facade** – orchestrates multiple API calls, for example: create user → update permissions.
4. **Playwright fixture** – injects the service into tests and handles cleanup.
5. **Page Object** – encapsulates UI interactions such as login.
6. **Test data factory** – creates unique, reusable payloads.

This follows SOLID principles and uses common patterns: **Page Object Model**, **Adapter/Gateway**, **Facade/Service Layer**, **Dependency Injection**, and **Factory** for test data.

---

### 1. Define shared types

```typescript
// e2e/support/user.types.ts

export interface CreateUserPayload {
  username: string;
  email: string;
  [key: string]: unknown;
}

export interface PermissionPayload {
  role?: string;
  permissions?: string[];
  [key: string]: unknown;
}

export interface CreatedUserResponse {
  id: string;
}

export interface Credentials {
  username: string;
  password: string;
}

export interface ProvisionedUser extends Credentials {
  id: string;
}
```

---

### 2. Define an API port/interface

This keeps the service decoupled from Playwright-specific implementation details.

```typescript
// e2e/support/user-api.port.ts

import type {
  CreateUserPayload,
  CreatedUserResponse,
  PermissionPayload,
  Credentials,
} from "./user.types";

export interface UserApiPort {
  createUser(payload: CreateUserPayload): Promise<CreatedUserResponse>;

  updatePermissions(
    userId: string,
    payload: PermissionPayload
  ): Promise<Credentials>;

  deleteUser(userId: string): Promise<void>;
}
```

---

### 3. Implement the Playwright API adapter

This class is responsible only for HTTP communication.

```typescript
// e2e/support/playwright-user-api.adapter.ts

import type { APIRequestContext, APIResponse } from "@playwright/test";

import type {
  CreateUserPayload,
  CreatedUserResponse,
  PermissionPayload,
  Credentials,
} from "./user.types";

import type { UserApiPort } from "./user-api.port";

export class PlaywrightUserApiAdapter implements UserApiPort {
  constructor(private readonly request: APIRequestContext) {}

  async createUser(payload: CreateUserPayload): Promise<CreatedUserResponse> {
    const response = await this.request.post("/users/v2/create", {
      data: payload,
    });

    this.assertStatus(response, [201], "Create user");

    const body = (await response.json()) as { id?: unknown };

    if (!body || body.id === undefined || body.id === null) {
      throw new Error("Create user response did not contain a valid user id");
    }

    return {
      id: String(body.id),
    };
  }

  async updatePermissions(
    userId: string,
    payload: PermissionPayload
  ): Promise<Credentials> {
    const response = await this.request.post("/users/v2/permissions", {
      data: {
        ...payload,
        id: userId,
      },
    });

    this.assertStatus(response, [200], "Update user permissions");

    const body = (await response.json()) as {
      username?: unknown;
      password?: unknown;
    };

    if (
      !body ||
      typeof body.username !== "string" ||
      typeof body.password !== "string"
    ) {
      throw new Error(
        "Update permissions response did not contain valid credentials"
      );
    }

    return {
      username: body.username,
      password: body.password,
    };
  }

  async deleteUser(userId: string): Promise<void> {
    const response = await this.request.delete(
      `/users/v2/${encodeURIComponent(userId)}`
    );

    // Adjust statuses according to your API contract.
    // 404 is accepted here because cleanup may run after a test already deleted the user.
    this.assertStatus(response, [200, 204, 404], "Delete user");
  }

  private assertStatus(
    response: APIResponse,
    expectedStatuses: readonly number[],
    operation: string
  ): void {
    if (!expectedStatuses.includes(response.status())) {
      throw new Error(
        `${operation} failed. Expected status ${expectedStatuses.join(
          " or "
        )}, but received ${response.status()}.`
      );
    }
  }
}
```

---

### 4. Create a provisioning service / facade

This class orchestrates the multiple API calls needed to create a usable test user.

```typescript
// e2e/support/user-provisioning.service.ts

import type {
  CreateUserPayload,
  PermissionPayload,
  ProvisionedUser,
} from "./user.types";

import type { UserApiPort } from "./user-api.port";

export class UserProvisioningService {
  private readonly createdUserIds = new Set<string>();

  constructor(private readonly userApi: UserApiPort) {}

  async createUserWithPermissions(
    createPayload: CreateUserPayload,
    permissionPayload: PermissionPayload
  ): Promise<ProvisionedUser> {
    const createdUser = await this.userApi.createUser(createPayload);

    // Track immediately so cleanup can still run if permission update fails.
    this.createdUserIds.add(createdUser.id);

    const credentials = await this.userApi.updatePermissions(
      createdUser.id,
      permissionPayload
    );

    return {
      id: createdUser.id,
      username: credentials.username,
      password: credentials.password,
    };
  }

  async createAdminUser(
    createPayload: CreateUserPayload
  ): Promise<ProvisionedUser> {
    return this.createUserWithPermissions(createPayload, {
      role: "admin",
      permissions: ["ADMIN"],
    });
  }

  async cleanup(): Promise<void> {
    const ids = [...this.createdUserIds];
    this.createdUserIds.clear();

    const results = await Promise.allSettled(
      ids.map((id) => this.userApi.deleteUser(id))
    );

    const failures = results.flatMap((result, index) =>
      result.status === "rejected"
        ? [
            {
              userId: ids[index],
              error: result.reason,
            },
          ]
        : []
    );

    if (failures.length > 0) {
      // You can also throw here if cleanup failures should fail the test.
      console.warn("Failed to clean up some test users", failures);
    }
  }
}
```

---

### 5. Wire the service through a Playwright fixture

This gives each test an isolated provisioner and guarantees cleanup.

```typescript
