## My answer

Below is a complete Arduino Nano implementation for your model-house lighting project.

I am assuming:

- Arduino Nano, ATmega328P, 5 V.
- External RTC is a DS3231. If it is a DS1307 or another RTC, the code needs a small library/class change.
- 0.96 inch I²C OLED, 128x64 SSD1306. Some 0.96 inch OLEDs are SH1106; those need a different constructor.
- You want the simulation to run continuously until power is removed.
- You mention “pico” once. I am treating this as “until the device is turned off.” If you actually mean a Raspberry Pi Pico, the same logic works, but the pin numbers and libraries differ.

I interpret “turn the night time code off / reduce to 1 hr” as a short-night mode: the normal bedtime TV remains 22:00 to 00:00, then the dark overnight period is reduced to 1 model hour, after which the simulation jumps to 06:00. If you instead want the bedroom TV itself shortened to 1 hour, I note where to change that.

---

# 1. What the project does

At the default 6:1 ratio:

| Simulated time | Real time |
|---|---:|
| 1 minute | 10 seconds |
| 1 hour | 10 minutes |
| 2 hours bedroom TV | 20 minutes |
| 24-hour day | 4 hours |

The house schedule is:

| Time | Activity |
|---|---|
| 00:00–06:00 | Night, all off. In short-night mode this becomes 00:00–01:00, then jumps to 06:00 |
| 06:00–07:00 | Bedroom TV morning news |
| 06:30–08:30 | Living-room light |
| 06:30–08:00 | Kitchen light |
| 06:15–06:45 | Bathroom light |
| 06:00–06:30 | Hall light |
| 08:30–17:30 | House mostly off / away |
| 17:30–22:00 | Living-room light |
| 17:30–19:30 | Kitchen light |
| 19:00–22:00 | Living-room TV |
| 21:30–22:00 | Bedroom light before bed |
| 22:00–00:00 | Bedroom TV only, lights off |

The OLED shows:

- simulated clock time,
- simulation speed ratio,
- night mode,
- current event name,
- TV status.

---

# 2. Parts

- Arduino Nano.
- DS3231 RTC module with backup battery.
- 0.96 inch SSD1306 I²C OLED, 128x64.
- LEDs for room lights.
- Two LEDs for TV simulation, preferably cool-white or blueish.
- Resistors for LEDs. For 5 V Nano outputs, 470 Ω is a safe starting value for typical LEDs.
- One pushbutton for changing speed ratio.
- One switch or pushbutton for short-night mode.
- 5 V power supply or USB.
- Breadboard/stripboard/wiring.

If you are driving more than one LED per room, use a transistor or MOSFET driver. Do not power many LEDs directly from one Nano pin.

---

# 3. Wiring

## I²C devices

Both the OLED and DS3231 connect to the Nano I²C bus.

| Nano pin | OLED | DS3231 |
|---|---|---|
| A4 | SDA | SDA |
| A5 | SCL | SCL |
| 5V | VCC, if module is 5 V compatible | VCC |
| GND | GND | GND |

Many SSD1306 OLED modules use I²C address `0x3C`. Some use `0x3D`.

The DS3231 usually uses address `0x68`.

Leave DS3231 `SQW` and `32K` pins disconnected unless you need them.

## Room lights and TV LEDs

| Function | Nano pin |
|---|---:|
| Living-room light | D4 |
| Bedroom light | D5 |
| Kitchen light | D6 |
| Bathroom light | D7 |
| Hall light | D8 |
| Living-room TV | D9 |
| Bedroom TV | D10 |

D9 and D10 are PWM pins, which allows the TV LEDs to flicker with variable brightness.

For each LED:

```text
Nano pin ---- resistor ---- LED anode (+)
                           LED cathode (-) ---- GND
```

For a typical white LED with 5 V, a 470 Ω resistor gives a safe low current. If the LED is too dim, use a smaller resistor such as 330 Ω or 220 Ω, but stay within the LED and Nano pin current limits.

## Controls

| Function | Nano pin | Other side |
|---|---:|---|
| Short-night switch | D2 | GND |
| Speed button | D3 | GND |

The code uses internal pull-up resistors, so no external resistors are needed for these inputs.

- D2 open: normal night.
- D2 closed: short night.
- D3 press: change speed ratio.

