## My solution

The final length is fixed. Since we remove exactly `k` elements while the length is greater than `k`, the remaining length is

```cpp
len = (n - 1) % k + 1;
```

This is `n % k`, except that when `n % k == 0` it becomes `k`.

### Key characterization

Let the original indices of the final remaining elements be

```text
p1 < p2 < ... < plen
```

Final survivors split the original array into gaps: before `p1`, between survivors, and after `plen`.

Every removal operation removes `k` consecutive currently-existing elements. Since final survivors are never removed, no operation can cross a final survivor. Therefore each operation deletes elements from exactly one gap. Hence every gap length must be divisible by `k`.

Conversely, if all gaps have lengths divisible by `k`, we can simply delete each gap in chunks of size `k`. So this condition is both necessary and sufficient.

Thus the `j`-th surviving element must have original index:

```text
pj = j + c * k
```

for some block number `c`, and these block numbers must be non-decreasing.

Equivalently, split the array into blocks of size `k`, except the last block has length `len`. The `j`-th final element must be chosen from the `j`-th position of one of those blocks.

### Binary search the median

For a candidate answer `x`, call an element good if it is `>= x`.

For an array of length `len`, its lower median is at least `x` iff at least

```cpp
need = len / 2 + 1
```

elements are good.

Now we only need to check whether we can choose a valid final subsequence containing at least `need` good elements.

Use DP:

```text
dp[i] = maximum number of good elements chosen among the first i final positions
```

Process blocks from left to right. Inside each block, process positions `1..len`.

For block starting at index `start`, position `j` corresponds to original index `start + j - 1`.

Transition:

```cpp
dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[j - 1] + good);
```

Increasing `j` allows multiple consecutive survivors to be taken from the same block, which is valid.

The check is monotonic, so binary search the maximum `x`.

### C++ code

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
